{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NGPm8ScTbxAkT5bv2SQpNR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good picture comparing APC 0 and APC 300 kernel profiles. Let me focus on the key differences:\n\n**Round 0 kernels (APC 0 vs APC 300):**\n- `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel<1,0>`: APC 0: 30.5ms (137 inst), APC 300: 221ms (539 inst)\n- `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel<0,0>`: APC 0: 3.3ms (78 inst), APC 300: 20ms (196 inst)\n\n\n- `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel<1,0>`: APC 0: 4.5ms (38 inst), APC 300: 196ms (396 inst)\n- `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel<0,0>`: APC 0: 7.2ms (177 inst), APC 300: 29ms (339 inst)\n\nThe pattern is clear: the coset-parallel kernels show dramatic slowdowns at APC 300, while the larger non-coset-parallel kernels remain relatively consistent between the two configurations. Looking at the breakdown of the logup interaction kernels, I'm seeing they contribute significantly to the Round 0 GPU time at APC 0, with the `<3,1,0>` variant taking the most time at 21.1ms, while the others range from 4-7ms each. Looking at the performance metrics for the remaining kernels, I'm seeing that the non-coset-parallel Round 0 at APC 300 totals around 78ms. When I add this to the APC 0 measurements of ~170ms for non-coset-parallel and ~46ms for coset-parallel, I get roughly 216ms total GPU time, but the actual measured Round 0 is only 173ms—which makes sense because these kernels overlap across segments and many of the larger ones are duplicated across different AIRs in each segment.\n\nThe discrepancy between my calculated kernel time and the measured time suggests either some kernels are being counted multiple times across different NVTX scopes, or the GPU is achieving good pipelining. But since we're using a single stream, there shouldn't be overlap. I think the issue is that the kernel counts I'm looking at span the entire run, not just Round 0—kernels like `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_kernel` appear in both Round 0 and the MLE rounds, so I'd need to filter by NVTX data to isolate just Round 0.\n\nThe clearer pattern is emerging at APC 300, where the coset-parallel Round 0 kernels are totaling around 466ms.\n\nAt APC 0, those same kernel types use only ~46ms across 430 instances, giving a 10.1x ratio that roughly aligns with the increase in AIR instances per segment (APC 300 has ~312 per segment versus ~20 at APC 0). The GPU work per instance is actually lower at APC 300 because the smaller AIRs added by APCs have fewer constraints, so while the per-kernel time is lower, the sheer count is much higher.\n\nThis points to the core bottleneck: Round 0 at APC 300 launches roughly 935 small coset-parallel kernels, each occupying only about 5% of the GPU's 108 streaming multiprocessors.  These run serially on a single CUDA stream, leaving the GPU 95% idle between kernel launches.\n\nThe best fix would be to parallelize Round 0 across multiple concurrent CUDA streams instead of one. By distributing the small AIRs round-robin across 8-16 streams, the scheduler could execute kernels from different streams simultaneously, packing the GPU much more efficiently. Since each AIR's kernels use only 4-12 blocks (3-11% of SMs), running 8 streams concurrently would fill 40-80% of the GPU, potentially dropping coset-parallel kernel time from ~466ms down to 60-120ms. The larger non-coset-parallel kernels already saturate the GPU, so they wouldn't benefit from this change.\n\nThis beats batching because it avoids memory constraints, doesn't require new kernel implementations, lets the CUDA scheduler handle distribution automatically, and is straightforward to implement with stream management in Rust. The projected impact is substantial: Round 0 APC 300 could drop from 569ms to 200-300ms, while Round 0 APC 0 would see minimal improvement since it's dominated by the already-saturated kernels. Overall STARK time excluding trace would improve by 270-370ms. The main risk is that stream overhead—creation, memory partitioning, synchronization—could eat into the gains if not managed carefully.\n\nNow I need to verify whether the VPMM memory manager from openvm-cuda-common is stream-aware, since moving to multi-stream execution requires allocations and kernel launches to happen on the same stream. I should also examine how `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` scales at different APC levels to understand where the remaining bottlenecks are.\n\nLooking at the kernel metrics, the `<0>` variant (without shared memory) actually got faster from 104ms to 54ms, while `<1>` (with shared memory) exploded from 33ms to 374ms—an 11x increase. This tells me that at APC 0, larger AIRs use the no-shared-mem path, but at APC 300, the many small AIRs are mostly hitting the shared-mem variant. The total GKR input evaluation time went from 137ms to 428ms (3.1x), but the number of AIR instances grew from 99 to 623, so the per-instance cost is actually improving even though total time increased.\n\nWhat's interesting is that the measured LogUp GKR time went down from 991ms to 792ms despite more GPU time being spent on input evaluation. That's because the GKR sumcheck—the expensive part—scales with the actual computational work, not the number of AIRs. Looking at the sumcheck kernels at APC 0, I see the round computation kernel taking 126ms across 238 instances, the bit reversal kernel at 91ms, and the multifold kernel at 85ms, with several other kernels contributing to the total.\n\nAt APC 300, those same kernels are significantly faster: the round computation drops to 70ms across 141 instances, bit reversal to 50ms, and multifold to 47ms. The total GKR sumcheck GPU time improves from about 384ms down to 212ms—roughly a 1.8x speedup that aligns with the ~2x reduction in bus interaction messages. But then the GKR input evaluation GPU time actually increases, which is pulling the overall measured time in the opposite direction.\n\nThe input evaluation jumps from 137ms to 428ms at APC 300, which completely offsets the sumcheck gains. That leaves the total LogUp GKR at 792ms versus 991ms at APC 0—only a 1.25x improvement when I'd expect closer to 2x. The bottleneck is the same pattern as Round 0: many small kernels running sequentially for different AIRs instead of overlapping. I'm seeing that multi-stream execution could address both Round 0 and GKR input evaluation simultaneously if I apply the same pattern to both, though that's more complex to implement. I should probably tackle Round 0 first since it's the more straightforward target.\n\nActually, I'm second-guessing whether multi-stream is even the right solution here. The current Round 0 Phase 1 launches kernels serially on a single stream—two kernels per AIR. The real issue is that small AIRs produce tiny grids like 2×4 blocks, which only uses 7% of the 108 available SMs. Instead of multi-stream complexity, what if I just increase the grid dimensions to better saturate the GPU?\n\nBut that won't work—the grid size is determined by the problem size, not something I can arbitrarily increase. So I'm back to either batching multiple AIRs into one kernel, running kernels concurrently across streams, or doing both. The batching approach had OOM issues with shared memory, though a sub-batching variant might solve that. Multi-stream is simpler conceptually since it doesn't require kernel changes, but it needs proper stream infrastructure. Let me dig into what stream management already exists in the codebase and how Round 0 is currently structured.\n\nI should also look at the GKR input evaluation path since that's another scaling bottleneck.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11433,"cache_read_input_tokens":62004,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemera