{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MoKjNPjZzBpXRYCAwLziTS","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent! Now I have all the information I need. Let me create a comprehensive report.\n\n## Comprehensive Investigation Report: GPU Prover Performance Architecture\n\nBased on my thorough analysis of the stark-backend codebase, here's what I found regarding GPU prover performance optimization:\n\n### 1. ROUND 0 IMPLEMENTATION ARCHITECTURE\n\n**Location**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs` (lines 599-928)\n\n**Phase 1: GPU Kernel Launch (Lines 744-841)**\nThe function `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` implements a two-phase pattern:\n- Iterates over all present AIRs sequentially (lines 750-841)\n- For each AIR, launches TWO kernels per iteration:\n  1. `evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu()` - plain AIR constraints evaluation\n  2. `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu()` - LogUp interaction evaluation\n- Kernels are launched on the **default per-thread CUDA stream** (`cudaStreamPerThread`)\n- Device buffers (`zc_result`, `logup_result`) containing kernel outputs are collected without blocking\n- Input buffers (`d_main_parts`) are kept alive in vectors until Phase 2\n\n**Phase 2: Stream Synchronization & Host Transfer (Lines 843-924)**\n- **Explicit `current_stream_sync()` barrier** (line 844) - blocks before any D2H transfers\n- Releases input buffers after kernels complete (line 847)\n- Sequential D2H transfers (lines 851, 890) of result buffers from pending queue\n- CPU-side polynomial construction follows immediately per AIR\n\n**Critical Observation**: The Phase 1 loop contains SEQUENTIAL kernel launches - all kernels are queued on the same stream without any inter-stream concurrency.\n\n### 2. CUDA STREAM INFRASTRUCTURE\n\n**Location**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/stream.rs`\n\n**Current Architecture**:\n- Uses **single per-thread default stream** (`cudaStreamPerThread` = 0x02)\n- No stream pool or multi-stream management in place\n- Functions available but NOT utilized:\n  - `CudaStream::new()` - can create non-blocking CUDA streams\n  - `cudaStreamWaitEvent()` - can synchronize across streams via events\n  \n**`current_stream_sync()` Implementation** (line 81-83):\n```rust\npub fn current_stream_sync() -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n    check(unsafe { cudaStreamSynchronize(cudaStreamPerThread) })\n}\n```\nThis synchronizes only the default per-thread stream.\n\n**Stream-Related Infrastructure in VPMM** (Virtual Physical Memory Manager):\n- `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/vm_pool.rs` tracks stream IDs per allocation\n- `malloc_internal()` captures current stream ID (line 90: `current_stream_id()`)\n- `free_region_insert()` records a CudaEvent on the current stream (line 404-405)\n- Free regions are merged ONLY if on same stream ID (lines 390, 397)\n- This means **VPMM is stream-aware for deferred cleanup**, but currently all allocations happen on one stream\n\n### 3. DEVICEBUFFER ALLOCATION & STREAM AWARENESS\n\n**Location**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-common/src/d_buffer.rs`\n\n**Key Finding**: DeviceBuffer allocations are NOT stream-aware at the DeviceBuffer level:\n- `with_capacity()` (line 60-82) calls `d_malloc()` which routes to VPMM\n- VPMM captures the **current stream ID** at allocation time (not explicit parameter)\n- For buffers < page_size: uses `cudaMallocAsync(cudaStreamPerThread)` - hardcoded to default stream\n- For buffers >= page_size: VPMM tracks stream_id via `current_stream_id()`\n- Fills allocated buffers with `cudaMemsetAsync(cudaStreamPerThread)` - always default stream\n\n**Memory Copy Operations** (lines 102-125):\n- D2H transfers in `to_host()` use `cudaMemcpyAsync(cudaStreamPerThread)` \n- All transfers happen on default stream\n- Includes explicit event recording and wait: `COPY_EVENT.record_and_wait(cudaStreamPerThread)`\n\n### 4. LOGUP GKR INPUT EVALUATION\n\n**Location**: `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs` (lines 88-221)\n\n**Pattern Matches Round 0**:\n- Also iterates over trace_interactions sequentially (line 108: `for meta in trace_interactions.iter().flatten()`)\n- Launches ONE kernel per AIR: `logup_gkr_input_eval()` (line 178)\n- Buffers written directly to shared output (`leaves`)\n- NO explicit sync between per-AIR kernel launches\n- Results are accumulated in-place during loop\n\n**Key Difference**: No explicit Phase 2 sync - kernel results are consumed during the loop itself.\n\n### 5. MULTI-STREAM FEASIBILITY ASSESSMENT\n\n**Current State: NOT FEASIBLE without significant changes**\n\n**Why concurrent execution won't work with current infrastructure**:\n\n1. **Hard-coded Stream Dependencies**\n   - All D2H transfers hardcoded to `cudaStreamPerThread` (copy.rs)\n   - All memsets hardcoded to `cudaStreamPerThread` (d_buffer.rs)\n   - No abstraction to specify target stream for operations\n\n2. **VPMM Stream Tracking Mismatch**\n   - VPMM tracks stream_id at allocation time via `current_stream_id()`\n   - Free regions recorded with stream-specific events\n   - If kernel launches happen on stream A but free on stream B, VPMM won't merge regions (line 390: requires same stream_id)\n   - Could lead to fragmentation if mixing streams\n\n3. **Memory Copy Event Synchronization**\n   - D2H uses shared global `COPY_EVENT` (copy.rs, line 12)\n   - `to_host()` calls `record_and_wait()` on same event\n   - Concurrent D2H transfers would overwrite pending event state\n\n4. **DeviceBuffer Lifetime Management**\n   - `d_main_parts_vec` kept alive explicitly during Phase 1 to prevent early deallocation\n   - With concurrent kernels, buffer lifetime becomes complex across multiple streams\n   - No reference counting or async lifetime tracking\n\n### 6. CALL CHAIN ANALYSIS\n\n**Proving Entry Point** → `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys()` in mod.rs (line 225)\n\n```\nprove() \n  ↓\nlog_gkr_input_evals() [per-AIR sequential loop, no Phase 2 sync]\n  ↓\nfractional_sumcheck_gpu() [GKR evaluation]\n  ↓\nsumcheck_uni_round0_polys() [Round 0 - PRIMARY BOTTLENECK]\n  ├─ Phase 1: For each AIR:\n  │  ├─ evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu() → zerocheck_ntt_eval_constraints() kernel\n  │  └─ evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu() → logup_bary_eval_interactions_round0() kernel\n  │\n  └─ Phase 2:\n     ├─ current_stream_sync() [BLOCKING BARRIER]\n     ├─ D2H transfers (to_host() on all result buffers)\n     └─ CPU polynomial construction\n```\n\n### 7. OPTIMIZATION OPPORTUNITIES & REQUIRED CHANGES\n\n**To Enable Multi-Stream Concurrent Execution**:\n\n1. **Create Stream Pool Abstraction**\n   - Implement `StreamPool` managing N non-blocking CUDA streams\n   - Round-robin assignment of AIRs to streams in Phase 1\n\n2. **Parameterize All Stream Operations**\n   - Modify `MemCopyH2D::to_device()` to accept optional stream parameter\n   - Modify `MemCopyD2H::to_host()` to accept optional stream parameter\n   - Update `DeviceBuffer::fill_zero()` to use specified stream\n   - Parameterize VPMM allocation/free with target stream\n\n3. **Implement Async Event-Based Synchronization**\n   - Instead of `current_stream_sync()`, use per-stream events\n   - Create CudaEvent per stream in pool\n   - Synchronize only needed dependencies, not entire device\n\n4. **Fix VPMM Region Merging**\n   - Allow region merging across streams with proper event chaining\n   - OR: Allocate stream-specific sub-pools to avoid cross-stream merging issues\n   - Document that concurrent streams can impact memory fragmentation\n\n5. **Buffer Lifetime & Reference Counting**\n   - Implement Arc-based reference counting for device buffers\n   - Allow safe sharing across multiple concurrent kernel launches\n   - Ensure proper deallocation only after all dependent kernels complete\n\n6. **Fix Event Reuse Issue**\n   - Replace shared global `COPY_EVENT` in copy.rs with thread-local or per-stream events\n   - Ensure no race conditions with concurrent D2H transfers\n\n### 8. KEY ARCHITECTURAL CONSTRAINTS\n\n- **No explicit stream parameter in extern \"C\" CUDA kernel declarations** - kernels implicitly use default stream\n- **VPMM deeply integrated with stream tracking** - changing allocation strategy requires architectural refactor\n- **Single global mutex over MemoryManager** - concurrent allocation/free from multiple threads would serialize anyway\n- **Async-free through VPMM** - frees are stream-ordered, but only if kernels are on same stream\n\n### SUMMARY\n\nThe GPU prover exhibits a clear **sequential per-AIR processing pattern** with a global stream synchronization barrier. While VPMM has some stream awareness for deferred cleanup, the kernel launch infrastructure is hardcoded to the default stream. \n\n**Multi-stream execution is theoretically feasible but requires:**\n- Creating a stream pool abstraction layer\n- Parameterizing all device operations (allocation, copy, memset) with stream targeting\n- Fixing the VPMM merging logic for cross-stream scenarios\n- Implementing proper async synchronization with per-stream events\n- Handling the shared event bug in D2H transfers\n\nThe bottleneck in Round 0 comes not just from individual kernel execution time, but from the sequential launch pattern and the blocking synchronization point between Phase 1 and Phase 2."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1de075eb-7395-4d25-b825-c548ab1430ba","uuid":"ea9bec4e-9081-47ef-b7ce-3020ad0dd06f","timestamp":"2026-04-11T08:25:54.270Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I'm investigating GPU prover performance in the stark-backend project. I need to understand the following areas to evaluate optimization approaches:\n\n1. **Round 0 implementation**: The main bottleneck. File is `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`. I need to understand how `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` works, particularly:\n   - How Phase 1 (GPU launch) iterates over AIR ins