{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MDBqCgt5CvzLrC3eRHrpKx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Task: 2026-04-11-0945-precompute-round0-logup-rules\n2\t\n3\tTask name: 2026-04-11-0945-precompute-round0-logup-rules\n4\t\n5\t## Problem Statement\n6\t\n7\tRound 0 constraint evaluation at APC 300 takes ~569ms — the single largest scaling bottleneck. While APC 300 has 2.15x fewer constraint instances than APC 0 (505M vs 1.13B), Round 0 at APC 300 is 3.25x SLOWER than APC 0 (569ms vs 175ms), contributing +394ms of inverse scaling.\n8\t\n9\tThe root cause: for each of the ~370 AIR instances with interactions (per segment, 2 segments), the Round 0 logup code path rebuilds the symbolic expression DAG and compiles GPU evaluation rules from scratch. This CPU-intensive work depends only on the AIR's constraint structure (fixed at keygen time) but is recomputed on every `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` call.\n10\t\n11\tProfiling data shows Round 0 GPU kernel time is ~479ms at APC 300 (baseline nsight), while total Round 0 time is 569ms post-d2h-sync. The ~90ms gap is CPU overhead: DAG construction, rule compilation, weight computation, and H2D transfers. However, the actual CPU overhead is larger because the pipeline masks some of it — GPU utilization drops when per-AIR CPU setup (0.3-0.6ms) exceeds per-AIR GPU kernel time (0.2-0.4ms for small AIRs).\n12\t\n13\tCompounding the issue: when batching is disabled (as for the pairing benchmark with large `skip_domain`), each AIR's DAG and rules are built TWICE:\n14\t1. In `mod.rs` Phase 1 loop (lines 907-926) — computing rules to check batch eligibility\n15\t2. In `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` (round0.rs lines 219-265) — rebuilding the same DAG and rules for the actual kernel launch\n16\t\n17\tThe Phase 1 computation is completely wasted for non-batch-eligible AIRs. This duplicate work adds ~0.2ms per AIR × 370 AIRs × 2 segments = ~148ms of pure waste.\n18\t\n19\tAdditionally, the Phase 2 IDFT processing loop (mod.rs lines 1184-1281) processes all 623 AIR results sequentially. Each iteration performs transpose + IDFT operations that are CPU-bound and embarrassingly parallel (each AIR writes to independent `batch_sp_poly` indices).\n20\t\n21\t**Note**: The batch-round0-coset-parallel commit (74134b4e, most recent) appears to cause a severe performance regression — the pairing benchmark fails to complete within 2 minutes. This commit should be investigated and likely reverted before applying this optimization. The measurements in this task reference the post-stacked-reduction state (commit 6fccffce).\n22\t\n23\t## Proposed Approach\n24\t\n25\t### Part 1: Pre-compute Round 0 logup interaction rules at keygen time\n26\t\n27\tIn `AirDataGpu::new()` (pkey.rs), pre-compile the Round 0 logup interaction rules alongside the existing `interaction_rules`:\n28\t\n29\t1. **Add `round0_interaction_rules` to `AirDataGpu`**: Build the interaction DAG with `buffer_vars=true` (as the Round 0 kernel requires) and compile rules via `SymbolicRulesGpu::new(&dag, true)`. Store the compiled rules, encoded rules on device, and a mapping from interaction index → rule index for fast weight computation.\n30\t\n31\t2. **Refactor `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu`** (round0.rs): Accept pre-compiled rules as a parameter instead of rebuilding from scratch. Remove the internal DAG construction and rule compilation (lines 219-265). Only compute runtime-dependent weights (numer_weights, denom_weights) using the pre-computed index mapping.\n32\t\n33\t3. **Simplify Phase 1 loop in `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`** (mod.rs): When `batching_enabled` is false, skip the DAG/rule construction entirely (lines 907-926) and go directly to `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` with pre-compiled rules. When batching IS enabled, use the pre-compiled rules for batch metadata instead of recompiling.\n34\t\n35\t### Part 2: Parallelize Phase 2 IDFT processing\n36\t\n37\tConvert the sequential Phase 2 loop (mod.rs lines 1184-1281) to use `rayon::par_iter()`:\n38\t\n39\t1. **Pre-fetch all D2H data** before entering the parallel section. For non-batched AIRs that still have `DeviceBuffer` results, call `to_host()` before the parallel loop (D2H transfers need the CUDA context).\n40\t\n41\t2. **Parallel IDFT processing**: Use `pending.into_par_iter()` to process all AIR results concurrently. Each iteration writes to independent indices in `batch_sp_poly` (via `entry.trace_idx`), so no synchronization is needed beyond the atomic slice access.\n42\t\n43\t## Expected Outcome\n44\t\n45\t**Round 0 improvement (APC 300)**:\n46\t- Eliminating duplicate DAG/rule compilation: ~100-150ms savings\n47\t  - Phase 1 loop waste (mod.rs): ~74ms/segment × 2 = 148ms\n48\t  - Round 0 function compilation (round0.rs): savings partially masked by GPU pipeline\n49\t- Net Round 0 reduction: ~100-150ms (from ~569ms to ~419-469ms)\n50\t- At APC 0 (99 AIR instances): minimal savings (~5-15ms) — optimization primarily helps APC 300\n51\t\n52\t**Phase 2 IDFT parallelization (APC 300)**:\n53\t- Sequential IDFT for 623 AIRs → parallel on 8+ cores\n54\t- Expected savings: 30-50ms\n55\t\n56\t**Combined STARK excl trace improvement (APC 300)**:\n57\t- Total: ~130-200ms reduction (from ~2195ms to ~1995-2065ms)\n58\t- Scaling ratio: from ~1.014 to ~0.95-0.97 (APC 300 becomes meaningfully faster than APC 0)\n59\t\n60\t## Success Criteria\n61\t\n62\t1. **Primary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 decreases by ≥100ms vs pre-optimization baseline (2195ms → ≤2095ms)\n63\t2. **Secondary**: Round 0 APC 300 decreases by ≥80ms (569ms → ≤489ms)\n64\t3. **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 ratio for STARK excl trace ≤ 0.98 (APC 300 measurably faster)\n65\t4. **Correctness**: All 94 openvm-cuda-backend tests pass; proof verification succeeds for APC 0/100/300\n66\t5. **No regression at APC 0**: STARK excl trace APC 0 change ≤ ±20ms\n67\t\n68\t## Considered Alternatives\n69\t\n70\t### 1. Batch GKR input evaluation kernels\n71\t- **Idea**: Batch the per-AIR `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` launches (791 instances at APC 300) into fewer large launches, similar to the Round 0 batching approach.\n72\t- **Expected impact**: ~200-300ms (nsight data from 003 directory shows 5x GPU time reduction: 428ms → 86ms)\n73\t- **Why not chosen**: Complex implementation requiring new CUDA kernels + Rust orchestration. Same memory constraint challenges as batch-round0 (which itself appears to cause a regression). The GKR input eval already uses pre-compiled rules from the proving key, so the redundant compilation savings don't apply here. Higher risk for uncertain return — the 003 directory metrics show STARK excl trace barely improved despite the GPU time reduction, suggesting hidden CPU overhead offsets.\n74\t\n75\t### 2. Multi-stream Round 0 pipelining\n76\t- **Idea**: Use 2-4 CUDA streams to overlap GPU kernel execution with CPU Phase 2 IDFT processing.\n77\t- **Expected impact**: 50-100ms savings\n78\t- **Why not chosen**: Complex synchronization logic, risk of race conditions with VPMM memory management. The Phase 2 IDFT parallelization achieves similar savings with much less complexity (rayon vs CUDA stream management).\n79\t\n80\t### 3. Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels\n81\t- **Idea**: Similar to the degenerate stacked reduction batching, batch the 7,467 per-window non-degenerate kernel launches.\n82\t- **Expected impact**: ~50-80ms on Stacked Reduction (from 147ms)\n83\t- **Why not chosen**: Lower absolute impact than Round 0 optimization. Stacked Reduction already improved 2x. Diminishing returns.\n84\t\n85\t### 4. Rayon-only Phase 2 IDFT (without rule pre-computation)\n86\t- **Idea**: Just parallelize the Phase 2 loop without addressing the redundant compilation.\n87\t- **Expected impact**: 30-50ms only\n88\t- **Why not chosen**: Included as Part 2 of the chosen approach. On its own, the savings are too small to justify a full optimization iteration. Combining with Part 1 gives meaningful total impact.\n89\t\n90\t### 5. Investigate and fix batch-round0 regression\n91\t- **Idea**: Debug why commit 74134b4e causes the pairing benchmark to hang.\n92\t- **Why not chosen as primary**: This is a bug fix/investigation, not a new optimization. Should be done as a prerequisite step during the Execute phase (likely a revert), not as the optimization itself.\n93\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1de075eb-7395-4d25-b825-c548ab1430ba","uuid":"4ecf652d-dfc1-4380-a847-ef0e6937508f","timestamp":"2026-04-11T08:31:05.644Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# Task: 2026-04-11-0945-precompute-round0-logup-rules\n\nTask name: 2026-04-11-0945-precompute-round0-logup-rules\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nRound 0 constraint evaluation at APC 300 takes ~569ms — the single largest scaling bottleneck. While APC 300 has 2.15x fewer constraint instances than APC 0 (505M vs 1.13B), Round 0 at APC 300 is 3.25x SLOWER than APC 0 (569ms vs 175ms), contributing +394ms of inverse scaling.\n\nThe root cause: for each of the ~370 AIR instances with interactions (per segment, 2 segments), the Round 0 logup code path rebuilds the symbolic expression DAG and compiles GPU evaluation rules from scratch. This CPU-intensive work depends only on the AIR's constraint structure (fixed at keygen time) but is recomputed on every `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` call.\n\nProfiling data shows Round 0 GPU kernel time is ~479ms at APC 300 (baseline nsight), while total Round 0 time is 569ms post-d2h-sync. The ~90ms gap is CPU overhead: DAG construction, rule compilation, weight computation, and H2D transfers. However, the actual CPU overhead is larger because the pipeline masks some of it — GPU utilization drops when per-AIR CPU setup (0.3-0.6ms) exceeds per-AIR GPU kernel time (0.2-0.4ms for small AIRs).\n\nCompounding the issue: when batching is disabled (as for the pairing benchmark with large `skip_domain`), each AIR's DAG and rules are built TWICE:\n1. In `mod.rs` Phase 1 loop (lines 907-926) — computing rules to check batch eligibility\n2. In `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` (round0.rs lines 219-265) — rebuilding the same DAG and rules for the actual kernel launch\n\nThe Phase 1 computation is completely wasted for non-batch-eligible AIRs. This duplicate work adds ~0.2ms per AIR × 370 AIRs × 2 segments = ~148ms of pure waste.\n\nAdditionally, the Phase 2 IDFT processing loop (mod.rs lines 1184-1281) processes all 623 AIR results sequentially. Each iteration performs transpose + IDFT operations that are CPU-bound and embarrassingly parallel (each AIR writes to independent `batch_sp_poly` indices).\n\n**Note**: The batch-round0-coset-parallel commit (74134b4e, most recent) appears to cause a severe performance regression — the pairing benchmark fails to complete within 2 minutes. This commit should be investigated and likely reverted before applying this optimization. The measurements in this task reference the post-stacked-reduction state (commit 6fccffce).\n\n## Proposed Approach\n\n### Part 1: Pre-compute Round 0 logup interaction rules at keygen time\n\nIn `AirDataGpu::new()` (pkey.rs), pre-compile the Round 0 logup interaction rules alongside the existing `interaction_rules`:\n\n1. **Add `round0_interaction_rules` to `AirDataGpu`**: Build the interaction DAG with `buffer_vars=true` (as the Round 0 kernel requires) and compile rules via `SymbolicRulesGpu::new(&dag, true)`. Store the compiled rules, encoded rules on device, and a mapping from interaction index → rule index for fast weight computation.\n\n2. **Refactor `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu`** (round0.rs): Accept pre-compiled rules as a parameter instead of rebuilding from scratch. Remove the internal DAG construction and rule compilation (lines 219-265). Only compute runtime-dependent weights (numer_weights, denom_weights) using the pre-computed index mapping.\n\n3. **Simplify Phase 1 loop in `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`** (mod.rs): When `batching_enabled` is false, skip the DAG/rule construction entirely (lines 907-926) and go directly to `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` with pre-compiled rules. When batching IS enabled, use the pre-compiled rules for batch metadata instead of recompiling.\n\n### Part 2: Parallelize Phase 2 IDFT processing\n\nConvert the sequential Phase 2 loop (mod.rs lines 1184-1281) to use `rayon::par_iter()`:\n\n1. **Pre-fetch all D2H data** before entering the parallel section. For non-batched AIRs that still have `DeviceBuffer` results, call `to_host()` before the parallel loop (D2H transfers need the CUDA context).\n\n2. **Parallel IDFT processing**: Use `pending.into_par_iter()` to process all AIR results concurrently. Each iteration writes to independent indices in `batch_sp_poly` (via `entry.trace_idx`), so no synchronization is needed beyond the atomic slice access.\n\n## Expected Outcome\n\n**Round 0 improvement (APC 300)**:\n- Eliminating duplicate DAG/rule compilation: ~100-150ms savings\n  - Phase 1 loop waste (mod.rs): ~74ms/segment × 2 = 148ms\n  - Round 0 function compilation (round0.rs): savings partially masked by GPU pipeline\n- Net Round 0 reduction: ~100-150ms (from ~569ms to ~419-469ms)\n- At APC 0 (99 AIR instances): minimal savings (~5-15ms) — optimization primarily helps APC 300\n\n**Phase 2 IDFT parallelization (APC 300)**:\n- Sequential IDFT for 623 AIRs → parallel on 8+ cores\n- Expected savings: 30-50ms\n\n**Combined STARK excl trace improvement (APC 300)**:\n- Total: ~130-200ms reduction (from ~2195ms to ~1995-2065ms)\n- Scaling ratio: from ~1.014 to ~0.95-0.97 (APC 300 becomes meaningfully faster than APC 0)\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n1. **Primary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 decreases by ≥100ms vs pre-optimization baseline (2195ms → ≤2095ms)\n2. **Secondary**: Round 0 APC 300 decreases by ≥80ms (569ms → ≤489ms)\n3. **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 ratio for STARK excl trace ≤ 0.98 (APC 300 measurably faster)\n4. **Correctness**: All 94 openvm-cuda-backend tests pass; proof verification succeeds for APC 0/100/300\n5. **No regression at APC 0**: STARK excl trace APC 0 change ≤ ±20ms\n\n## Considered Alternatives\n\n### 1. Batch GKR input evaluation kernels\n- **Idea**: Batch the per-AIR `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` launches (791 instances at APC 300) into fewer large launches, similar to the Round 0 batching approach.\n- **Expected impact**: ~200-300ms (nsight data from 003 directory shows 5x GPU time reduction: 428ms → 86ms)\n- **Why not chosen**: Complex implementation requiring new CUDA kernels + Rust orchestration. Same memory constraint challenges as batch-round0 (which itself appears to cause a regression). The GKR input eval already uses pre-compiled rules from the proving key, so the redundant compilation savings don't apply here. Higher risk for uncertain return — the 003 directory metrics show STARK excl trace barely improved despite the GPU time reduction, suggesting hidden CPU overhead offsets.\n\n### 2. Multi-stream Round 0 pipelining\n- **Idea**: Use 2-4 CUDA streams to overlap GPU kernel execution with CPU Phase 2 IDFT processing.\n- **Expected impact**: 50-100ms savings\n- **Why not chosen**: Complex synchronization logic, risk of race conditions with VPMM memory management. The Phase 2 IDFT parallelization achieves similar savings with much less complexity (rayon vs CUDA stream management).\n\n### 3. Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels\n- **Idea**: Similar to the degenerate stacked reduction batching, batch the 7,467 per-window non-degenerate kernel launches.\n- **Expected impact**: ~50-80ms on Stacked Reduction (from 147ms)\n- **Why not chosen**: Lower absolute impact than Round 0 optimization. Stacked Reduction already improved 2x. Diminishing returns.\n\n### 4. Rayon-only Phase 2 IDFT (without rule pre-computation)\n- **Idea**: Just parallelize the Phase 2 loop without addressing the redundant compilation.\n- **Expected impact**: 30-50ms only\n- **Why not chosen**: Included as Part 2 of the chosen approach. On its own, the savings are too small to justify a full optimization iteration. Combining with Part 1 gives meaningful total impact.\n\n### 5. Investigate and fix batch-round0 regression\n- **Idea**: Debug why commit 74134b4e causes the pairing benchmark to hang.\n- **Why not chosen as primary**: This is a bug fix/investigation, not a new optimization. Should be done as a prerequisite step during the Execute phase (likely a revert), not as the optimization itself.\n","numLines":93,"startLine":1,"totalLines":93}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XpeYK13pRcX3pKUbtbqiyD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EQeeD1N3zkmUR45f4psAoT","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# Task: Sub-batch Round 0 Coset-Parallel Kernel Launches\n\nTask name: 2026-04-11-1145-subbatch-round0-coset-parallel\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nRound 0 of the constraint evaluation phase at APC 300 launches ~1,470 small coset-parallel GPU kernels (539 zerocheck + 396 logup with shared memory, plus 196 + 339 without), each utilizing only 3-11% of the GPU's 128 SMs. These kernels execute serially on a single CUDA stream, leaving ~90% of GPU compute idle during each kernel.\n\nProfiling data (nsys, APC 300 current):\n- `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel<1,0>`: 221ms total, 539 instances, avg 410us\n- `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel<1,0>`: 196ms total, 396 instances, avg 494us\n- `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel<0,0>`: 20ms total, 196 instances, avg 101us\n- `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel<0,0>`: 29ms total, 339 instances, avg 85us\n- Total coset-parallel GPU time: 466ms out of 569ms Round 0 wall time\n\nBy comparison, at APC 0 (137+78+38+177 = 430 instances), the same kernel types total only 46ms. The 10x GPU time increase for ~3.4x more instances reflects severe SM underutilization: each small-AIR kernel launches only 4-12 thread blocks on a 128-SM GPU.\n\nRound 0 is the largest scaling bottleneck: it increases by +396ms from APC 0 (173ms) to APC 300 (569ms), accounting for the majority of the +67ms net STARK excl trace gap (2134ms vs 2201ms).\n\n## Proposed Approach\n\nBatch multiple small coset-parallel AIR instances into single kernel launches using sub-batching with a memory budget. This directly improves SM utilization by launching many more thread blocks per kernel call.\n\n**Key design: Sub-batching (not all-or-nothing)**\n\nA previous attempt (commit 74134b4e, reverted at 61dc6616) implemented all-or-nothing batching with a 128MB memory budget check that disabled batching entirely when any AIR had a large skip_domain. This optimization refines that approach:\n\n1. **Group AIRs into sub-batches** sized to fit within a memory budget (e.g., 64-128MB per batch). Sort coset-parallel AIRs by `skip_domain` and `num_cosets`, then greedily fill batches up to the budget. This means:\n   - Small-skip AIRs (the majority at APC 300): batches of 16-64 AIRs\n   - Large-skip AIRs: batches of 2-4 AIRs, or individual launches if too large\n\n2. **Reuse existing batched CUDA kernels** from commit 74134b4e. The kernels were verified correct (94/94 tests pass). They use a 2D grid `(total_x_blocks, max_num_cosets)` with per-AIR metadata structs (`ZerocheckBatchMeta`, `LogupBatchMeta`) and `air_for_block` mapping. The `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` reduction kernel handles multi-segment output.\n\n3. **Sub-batch by compatible parameters**: Group AIRs with the same `num_cosets` together to avoid coset-dimension padding waste. Within each group, limit batch size by total output buffer memory.\n\n4. **Mechanism for improvement**: A batch of 16 AIRs, each launching 8 thread blocks, produces a combined grid of 128 blocks -- enough to saturate the 128-SM RTX 4090. Instead of 16 serial kernels at 5-10% SM utilization, we get 1 kernel at ~100% utilization. The GPU completes the same work in ~1/16th the wall time.\n\n**What changes:**\n- CUDA: Re-introduce the batched coset-parallel kernels from 74134b4e (zerocheck + logup)\n- Rust: Replace the all-or-nothing batching logic with sub-batch grouping in Phase 1 of `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`. AIRs are classified, grouped into sub-batches, and each sub-batch gets a single kernel launch. Individual launches remain for non-eligible AIRs (large buffer_size, GLOBAL=true).\n\n## Expected Outcome\n\n- **Round 0 APC 300**: 569ms -> 200-300ms (3-5x reduction in coset-parallel GPU time through SM saturation, plus reduced launch overhead from ~1470 -> ~50-100 launches)\n- **STARK excl trace APC 300**: 2201ms -> ~1850-1950ms (270-370ms improvement)\n- **Scaling ratio (APC 300/APC 0)**: 1.031 -> ~0.88-0.92 (APC 300 becomes faster than APC 0 for STARK excl trace)\n- **APC 0 impact**: Minimal change (Round 0 at APC 0 is dominated by non-coset-parallel large-AIR kernels)\n- **Cumulative vs baseline APC 300**: 2478ms -> ~1850-1950ms (-21% to -25%)\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n- **Primary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 decreases by >= 150ms vs current (2201ms -> <= 2051ms)\n- **Secondary**: Round 0 APC 300 decreases by >= 30% (569ms -> <= 398ms)\n- **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 STARK excl trace ratio < 1.00 (currently 1.031)\n- **Correctness**: All 94 `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass; proof verification succeeds for all APC configs\n- **Rollback threshold**: If Round 0 APC 300 >= 540ms (within 5% of current), revert\n\n## Considered Alternatives\n\n1. **Multi-stream Round 0 execution** (concurrent kernels via multiple CUDA streams): Would achieve similar SM utilization improvement without new CUDA kernels. However, the current CUDA infrastructure hardcodes `cudaStreamPerThread` in DeviceBuffer allocation, D2H transfers, and memset operations. Enabling multi-stream requires deep cross-cutting changes to `cuda-common` (stream pool, parameterized stream in all CUDA calls, VPMM stream-aware allocation). Estimated 3x more complex than sub-batching, with similar expected improvement. **Rejected: too complex for this iteration.**\n\n2. **Batch LogUp GKR input evaluation kernels**: GKR input eval has 791 kernel launches at APC 300 totaling 428ms GPU time -- another per-AIR scaling bottleneck. However, Round 0 has the larger absolute scaling penalty (+396ms APC 0->300 vs -199ms for LogUp GKR) and has proven CUDA kernels already in git history. **Deferred: good candidate for next iteration.**\n\n3. **Pre-allocate reusable device buffers**: Eliminate per-AIR `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity` overhead in Round 0. Expected savings: 20-30ms (the overhead gap is only ~53ms, much of which is CUDA runtime dispatch, not malloc). **Rejected: too low impact to justify as standalone task.**\n\n4. **Batch calculate_zero_hash in Trace Commit**: 584 instances at 187us each = 109ms total. Batching would save 50-80ms. **Deferred: smaller impact, good future candidate.**\n\n5. **All-or-nothing Round 0 batching (repeat previous attempt)**: The memory budget check that disabled batching entirely for large skip_domains would still apply. The pairing benchmark likely triggers the check, making the optimization a no-op. **Rejected: sub-batching directly addresses this flaw.**\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2574,"cache_read_input_tokens":101289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2574},"output_tokens":65,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1de075eb-7395-4d25-b825-c548ab1430ba","uuid":"a7e8c46d-172c-44c7-b027-cdcec2b64414"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EQeeD1N3zkmUR45f4psAoT","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1de075eb-7395-4d25-b825-c548ab1430ba","uuid":"2b0adb20-8147-48ed-8ae0-1045a02ef1a8","timestamp":"2026-04-11T08:31:35.332Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# Task: Sub-batch Round 0 Coset-Parallel Kernel Launches\n\nTask name: 2026-04-11-1145-subbatch-round0-coset-parallel\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nRound 0 of the constraint evaluation phase at APC 300 launches ~1,470 small coset-parallel GPU kernels (539 zerocheck + 396 logup with shared memory, plus 196 + 339 without), each utilizing only 3-11% of the GPU's 128 SMs. These kernels execute serially on a single CUDA stream, leaving ~90% of GPU compute idle during each kernel.\n\nProfiling data (nsys, APC 300 current):\n- `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel<1,0>`: 221ms total, 539 instances, avg 410us\n- `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel<1,0>`: 196ms total, 396 instances, avg 494us\n- `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel<0,0>`: 20ms total, 196 instances, avg 101us\n- `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel<0,0>`: 29ms total, 339 instances, avg 85us\n- Total coset-parallel GPU time: 466ms out of 569ms Round 0 wall time\n\nBy comparison, at APC 0 (137+78+38+177 = 430 instances), the same kernel types total only 46ms. The 10x GPU time increase for ~3.4x more instances reflects severe SM underutilization: each small-AIR kernel launches only 4-12 thread blocks on a 128-SM GPU.\n\nRound 0 is the largest scaling bottleneck: it increases by +396ms from APC 0 (173ms) to APC 300 (569ms), accounting for the majority of the +67ms net STARK excl trace gap (2134ms vs 2201ms).\n\n## Proposed Approach\n\nBatch multiple small coset-parallel AIR instances into single kernel launches using sub-batching with a memory budget. This directly improves SM utilization by launching many more thread blocks per kernel call.\n\n**Key design: Sub-batching (not all-or-nothing)**\n\nA previous attempt (commit 74134b4e, reverted at 61dc6616) implemented all-or-nothing batching with a 128MB memory budget check that disabled batching entirely when any AIR had a large skip_domain. This optimization refines that approach:\n\n1. **Group AIRs into sub-batches** sized to fit within a memory budget (e.g., 64-128MB per batch). Sort coset-parallel AIRs by `skip_domain` and `num_cosets`, then greedily fill batches up to the budget. This means:\n   - Small-skip AIRs (the majority at APC 300): batches of 16-64 AIRs\n   - Large-skip AIRs: batches of 2-4 AIRs, or individual launches if too large\n\n2. **Reuse existing batched CUDA kernels** from commit 74134b4e. The kernels were verified correct (94/94 tests pass). They use a 2D grid `(total_x_blocks, max_num_cosets)` with per-AIR metadata structs (`ZerocheckBatchMeta`, `LogupBatchMeta`) and `air_for_block` mapping. The `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` reduction kernel handles multi-segment output.\n\n3. **Sub-batch by compatible parameters**: Group AIRs with the same `num_cosets` together to avoid coset-dimension padding waste. Within each group, limit batch size by total output buffer memory.\n\n4. **Mechanism for improvement**: A batch of 16 AIRs, each launching 8 thread blocks, produces a combined grid of 128 blocks -- enough to saturate the 128-SM RTX 4090. Instead of 16 serial kernels at 5-10% SM utilization, we get 1 kernel at ~100% utilization. The GPU completes the same work in ~1/16th the wall time.\n\n**What changes:**\n- CUDA: Re-introduce the batched coset-parallel kernels from 74134b4e (zerocheck + logup)\n- Rust: Replace the all-or-nothing batching logic with sub-batch grouping in Phase 1 of `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`. AIRs are classified, grouped into sub-batches, and each sub-batch gets a single kernel launch. Individual launches remain for non-eligible AIRs (large buffer_size, GLOBAL=true).\n\n## Expected Outcome\n\n- **Round 0 APC 300**: 569ms -> 200-300ms (3-5x reduction in coset-parallel GPU time through SM saturation, plus reduced launch overhead from ~1470 -> ~50-100 launches)\n- **STARK excl trace APC 300**: 2201ms -> ~1850-1950ms (270-370ms improvement)\n- **Scaling ratio (APC 300/APC 0)**: 1.031 -> ~0.88-0.92 (APC 300 becomes faster than APC 0 for STARK excl trace)\n- **APC 0 impact**: Minimal change (Round 0 at APC 0 is dominated by non-coset-parallel large-AIR kernels)\n- **Cumulative vs baseline APC 300**: 2478ms -> ~1850-1950ms (-21% to -25%)\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n- **Primary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 decreases by >= 150ms vs current (2201ms -> <= 2051ms)\n- **Secondary**: Round 0 APC 300 decreases by >= 30% (569ms -> <= 398ms)\n- **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 STARK excl trace ratio < 1.00 (currently 1.031)\n- **Correctness**: All 94 `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass; proof verification succeeds for all APC configs\n- **Rollback threshold**: If Round 0 APC 300 >= 540ms (within 5% of current), revert\n\n## Considered Alternatives\n\n1. **Multi-stream Round 0 execution** (concurrent kernels via multiple CUDA streams): Would achieve similar SM utilization improvement without new CUDA kernels. However, the current CUDA infrastructure hardcodes `cudaStreamPerThread` in DeviceBuffer allocation, D2H transfers, and memset operations. Enabling multi-stream requires deep cross-cutting changes to `cuda-common` (stream pool, parameterized stream in all CUDA calls, VPMM stream-aware allocation). Estimated 3x more complex than sub-batching, with similar expected improvement. **Rejected: too complex for this iteration.**\n\n2. **Batch LogUp GKR input evaluation kernels**: GKR input eval has 791 kernel launches at APC 300 totaling 428ms GPU time -- another per-AIR scaling bottleneck. However, Round 0 has the larger absolute scaling penalty (+396ms APC 0->300 vs -199ms for LogUp GKR) and has proven CUDA kernels already in git history. **Deferred: good candidate for next iteration.**\n\n3. **Pre-allocate reusable device buffers**: Eliminate per-AIR `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity` overhead in Round 0. Expected savings: 20-30ms (the overhead gap is only ~53ms, much of which is CUDA runtime dispatch, not malloc). **Rejected: too low impact to justify as standalone task.**\n\n4. **Batch calculate_zero_hash in Trace Commit**: 584 instances at 187us each = 109ms total. Batching would save 50-80ms. **Deferred: smaller impact, good future candidate.**\n\n5. **All-or-nothing Round 0 batching (repeat previous attempt)**: The memory budget check that disabled batching entirely for large skip_domains would still apply. The pairing benchmark likely triggers the check, making the optimization a no-op. **Rejected: sub-batching directly addresses this flaw.**\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":92,"newStart":1,"newLines":68,"lines":["-# Task: 2026-04-11-0945-precompute-round0-logup-rules","+# Task: Sub-batch Round 0 Coset-Parallel Kernel Launches"," ","-Task name: 2026-04-11-0945-precompute-round0-logup-rules","+Task name: 2026-04-11-1145-subbatch-round0-coset-parallel"," "," ## Problem Statement"," ","-Round 0 constraint evaluation at APC 300 takes ~569ms — the single largest scaling bottleneck. While APC 300 has 2.15x fewer constraint instances than APC 0 (505M vs 1.13B), Round 0 at APC 300 is 3.25x SLOWER than APC 0 (569ms vs 175ms), contributing +394ms of inverse scaling.","+Round 0 of the constraint evaluation phase at APC 300 launches ~1,470 small coset-parallel GPU kernels (539 zerocheck + 396 logup with shared memory, plus 196 + 339 without), each utilizing only 3-11% of the GPU's 128 SMs. These kernels execute serially on a single CUDA stream, leaving ~90% of GPU compute idle during each kernel."," ","-The root cause: for each of the ~370 AIR instances with interactions (per segment, 2 segments), the Round 0 logup code path rebuilds the symbolic expression DAG and compiles GPU evaluation rules from scratch. This CPU-intensive work depends only on the AIR's constraint structure (fixed at keygen time) but is recomputed on every `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` call.","+Profiling data (nsys, APC 300 current):","+- `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel<1,0>`: 221ms total, 539 instances, avg 410us","+- `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel<1,0>`: 196ms total, 396 instances, avg 494us","+- `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel<0,0>`: 20ms total, 196 instances, avg 101us","+- `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel<0,0>`: 29ms total, 339 instances, avg 85us","+- Total coset-parallel GPU time: 466ms out of 569ms Round 0 wall time"," ","-Profiling data shows Round 0 GPU kernel time is ~479ms at APC 300 (baseline nsight), while total Round 0 time is 569ms post-d2h-sync. The ~90ms gap is CPU overhead: DAG construction, rule compilation, weight computation, and H2D transfers. However, the actual CPU overhead is larger because the pipeline masks some of it — GPU utilization drops when per-AIR CPU setup (0.3-0.6ms) exceeds per-AIR GPU kernel time (0.2-0.4ms for small AIRs).","+By comparison, at APC 0 (137+78+38+177 = 430 instances), the same kernel types total only 46ms. The 10x GPU time increase for ~3.4x more instances reflects severe SM underutilization: each small-AIR kernel launches only 4-12 thread blocks on a 128-SM GPU."," ","-Compounding the issue: when batching is disabled (as for the pairing benchmark with large `skip_domain`), each AIR's DAG and rules are built TWICE:","-1. In `mod.rs` Phase 1 loop (lines 907-926) — computing rules to check batch eligibility","-2. In `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` (round0.rs lines 219-265) — rebuilding the same DAG and rules for the actual kernel launch","+Round 0 is the largest scaling bottleneck: it increases by +396ms from APC 0 (173ms) to APC 300 (569ms), accounting for the majority of the +67ms net STARK excl trace gap (2134ms vs 2201ms)."," ","-The Phase 1 computation is completely wasted for non-batch-eligible AIRs. This duplicate work adds ~0.2ms per AIR × 370 AIRs × 2 segments = ~148ms of pure waste.","-","-Additionally, the Phase 2 IDFT processing loop (mod.rs lines 1184-1281) processes all 623 AIR results sequentially. Each iteration performs transpose + IDFT operations that are CPU-bound and embarrassingly parallel (each AIR writes to independent `batch_sp_poly` indices).","-","-**Note**: The batch-round0-coset-parallel commit (74134b4e, most recent) appears to cause a severe performance regression — the pairing benchmark fails to complete within 2 minutes. This commit should be investigated and likely reverted before applying this optimization. The measurements in this task reference the post-stacked-reduction state (commit 6fccffce).","-"," ## Proposed Approach"," ","-### Part 1: Pre-compute Round 0 logup interaction rules at keygen time","+Batch multiple small coset-parallel AIR instances into single kernel launches using sub-batching with a memory budget. This directly improves SM utilization by launching many more thread blocks per kernel call."," ","-In `AirDataGpu::new()` (pkey.rs), pre-compile the Round 0 logup interaction rules alongside the existing `interaction_rules`:","+**Key design: Sub-batching (not all-or-nothing)**"," ","-1. **Add `round0_interaction_rules` to `AirDataGpu`**: Build the interaction DAG with `buffer_vars=true` (as the Round 0 kernel requires) and compile rules via `SymbolicRulesGpu::new(&dag, true)`. Store the compiled rules, encoded rules on device, and a mapping from interaction index → rule index for fast weight computation.","+A previous attempt (commit 74134b4e, reverted at 61dc6616) implemented all-or-nothing batching with a 128MB memory budget check that disabled batching entirely when any AIR had a large skip_domain. This optimization refines that approach:"," ","-2. **Refactor `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu`** (round0.rs): Accept pre-compiled rules as a parameter instead of rebuilding from scratch. Remove the internal DAG construction and rule compilation (lines 219-265). Only compute runtime-dependent weights (numer_weights, denom_weights) using the pre-computed index mapping.","+1. **Group AIRs into sub-batches** sized to fit within a memory budget (e.g., 64-128MB per batch). Sort coset-parallel AIRs by `skip_domain` and `num_cosets`, then greedily fill batches up to the budget. This means:","+   - Small-skip AIRs (the majority at APC 300): batches of 16-64 AIRs","+   - Large-skip AIRs: batches of 2-4 AIRs, or individual launches if too large"," ","-3. **Simplify Phase 1 loop in `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`** (mod.rs): When `batching_enabled` is false, skip the DAG/rule construction entirely (lines 907-926) and go directly to `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` with pre-compiled rules. When batching IS enabled, use the pre-compiled rules for batch metadata instead of recompiling.","+2. **Reuse existing batched CUDA kernels** from commit 74134b4e. The kernels were verified correct (94/94 tests pass). They use a 2D grid `(total_x_blocks, max_num_cosets)` with per-AIR metadata structs (`ZerocheckBatchMeta`, `LogupBatchMeta`) and `air_for_block` mapping. The `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` reduction kernel handles multi-segment output."," ","-### Part 2: Parallelize Phase 2 IDFT processing","+3. **Sub-batch by compatible parameters**: Group AIRs with the same `num_cosets` together to avoid coset-dimension padding waste. Within each group, limit batch size by total output buffer memory."," ","-Convert the sequential Phase 2 loop (mod.rs lines 1184-1281) to use `rayon::par_iter()`:","+4. **Mechanism for improvement**: A batch of 16 AIRs, each launching 8 thread blocks, produces a combined grid of 128 blocks -- enough to saturate the 128-SM RTX 4090. Instead of 16 serial kernels at 5-10% SM utilization, we get 1 kernel at ~100% utilization. The GPU completes the same work in ~1/16th the wall time."," ","-1. **Pre-fetch all D2H data** before entering the parallel section. For non-batched AIRs that still have `DeviceBuffer` results, call `to_host()` before the parallel loop (D2H transfers need the CUDA context).","+**What changes:**","+- CUDA: Re-introduce the batched coset-parallel kernels from 74134b4e (zerocheck + logup)","+- Rust: Replace the all-or-nothing batching logic with sub-batch grouping in Phase 1 of `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`. AIRs are classified, grouped into sub-batches, and each sub-batch gets a single kernel launch. Individual launches remain for non-eligible AIRs (large buffer_size, GLOBAL=true)."," ","-2. **Parallel IDFT processing**: Use `pending.into_par_iter()` to process all AIR results concurrently. Each iteration writes to independent indices in `batch_sp_poly` (via `entry.trace_idx`), so no synchronization is needed beyond the atomic slice access.","-"," ## Expected Outcome"," ","-**Round 0 improvement (APC 300)**:","-- Eliminating duplicate DAG/rule compilation: ~100-150ms savings","-  - Phase 1 loop waste (mod.rs): ~74ms/segment × 2 = 148ms","-  - Round 0 function compilation (round0.rs): savings partially masked by GPU pipeline","-- Net Round 0 reduction: ~100-150ms (from ~569ms to ~419-469ms)","-- At APC 0 (99 AIR instances): minimal savings (~5-15ms) — optimization primarily helps APC 300","+- **Round 0 APC 300**: 569ms -> 200-300ms (3-5x reduction in coset-parallel GPU time through SM saturation, plus reduced launch overhead from ~1470 -> ~50-100 launches)","+- **STARK excl trace APC 300**: 2201ms -> ~1850-1950ms (270-370ms improvement)","+- **Scaling ratio (APC 300/APC 0)**: 1.031 -> ~0.88-0.92 (APC 300 becomes faster than APC 0 for STARK excl trace)","+- **APC 0 impact**: Minimal change (Round 0 at APC 0 is dominated by non-coset-parallel large-AIR kernels)","+- **Cumulative vs baseline APC 300**: 2478ms -> ~1850-1950ms (-21% to -25%)"," ","-**Phase 2 IDFT parallelization (APC 300)**:","-- Sequential IDFT for 623 AIRs → parallel on 8+ cores","-- Expected savings: 30-50ms","-","-**Combined STARK excl trace improvement (APC 300)**:","-- Total: ~130-200ms reduction (from ~2195ms to ~1995-2065ms)","-- Scaling ratio: from ~1.014 to ~0.95-0.97 (APC 300 becomes meaningfully faster than APC 0)","-"," ## Success Criteria"," ","-1. **Primary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 decreases by ≥100ms vs pre-optimization baseline (2195ms → ≤2095ms)","-2. **Secondary**: Round 0 APC 300 decreases by ≥80ms (569ms → ≤489ms)","-3. **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 ratio for STARK excl trace ≤ 0.98 (APC 300 measurably faster)","-4. **Correctness**: All 94 openvm-cuda-backend tests pass; proof verification succeeds for APC 0/100/300","-5. **No regression at APC 0**: STARK excl trace APC 0 change ≤ ±20ms","+- **Primary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 decreases by >= 150ms vs current (2201ms -> <= 2051ms)","+- **Secondary**: Round 0 APC 300 decreases by >= 30% (569ms -> <= 398ms)","+- **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 STARK excl trace ratio < 1.00 (currently 1.031)","+- **Correctness**: All 94 `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass; proof verification succeeds for all APC configs","+- **Rollback threshold**: If Round 0 APC 300 >= 540ms (within 5% of current), revert"," "," ## Considered Alternatives"," ","-### 1. Batch GKR input evaluation kernels","-- **Idea**: Batch the per-AIR `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` launches (791 instances at APC 300) into fewer large launches, similar to the Round 0 batching approach.","-- **Expected impact**: ~200-300ms (nsight data from 003 directory shows 5x GPU time reduction: 428ms → 86ms)","-- **Why not chosen**: Complex implementation requiring new CUDA kernels + Rust orchestration. Same memory constraint challenges as batch-round0 (which itself appears to cause a regression). The GKR input eval already uses pre-compiled rules from the proving key, so the redundant compilation savings don't apply here. Higher risk for uncertain return — the 003 directory metrics show STARK excl trace barely improved despite the GPU time reduction, suggesting hidden CPU overhead offsets.","+1. **Multi-stream Round 0 execution** (concurrent kernels via multiple CUDA streams): Would achieve similar SM utilization improvement without new CUDA kernels. However, the current CUDA infrastructure hardcodes `cudaStreamPerThread` in DeviceBuffer allocation, D2H transfers, and memset operations. Enabling multi-stream requires deep cross-cutting changes to `cuda-common` (stream pool, parameterized stream in all CUDA calls, VPMM stream-aware allocation). Estimated 3x more complex than sub-batching, with similar expected improvement. **Rejected: too complex for this iteration.**"," ","-### 2. Multi-stream Round 0 pipelining","-- **Idea**: Use 2-4 CUDA streams to overlap GPU kernel execution with CPU Phase 2 IDFT processing.","-- **Expected impact**: 50-100ms savings","-- **Why not chosen**: Complex synchronization logic, risk of race conditions with VPMM memory management. The Phase 2 IDFT parallelization achieves similar savings with much less complexity (rayon vs CUDA stream management).","+2. **Batch LogUp GKR input evaluation kernels**: GKR input eval has 791 kernel launches at APC 300 totaling 428ms GPU time -- another per-AIR scaling bottleneck. However, Round 0 has the larger absolute scaling penalty (+396ms APC 0->300 vs -199ms for LogUp GKR) and has proven CUDA kernels already in git history. **Deferred: good candidate for next iteration.**"," ","-### 3. Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels","-- **Idea**: Similar to the degenerate stacked reduction batching, batch the 7,467 per-window non-degenerate kernel launches.","-- **Expected impact**: ~50-80ms on Stacked Reduction (from 147ms)","-- **Why not chosen**: Lower absolute impact than Round 0 optimization. Stacked Reduction already improved 2x. Diminishing returns.","+3. **Pre-allocate reusable device buffers**: Eliminate per-AIR `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity` overhead in Round 0. Expected savings: 20-30ms (the overhead gap is only ~53ms, much of which is CUDA runtime dispatch, not malloc). **Rejected: too low impact to justify as standalone task.**"," ","-### 4. Rayon-only Phase 2 IDFT (without rule pre-computation)","-- **Idea**: Just parallelize the Phase 2 loop without addressing the redundant compilation.","-- **Expected impact**: 30-50ms only","-- **Why not chosen**: Included as Part 2 of the chosen approach. On its own, the savings are too small to justify a full optimization iteration. Combining with Part 1 gives meaningful total impact.","+4. **Batch calculate_zero_hash in Trace Commit**: 584 instances at 187us each = 109ms total. Batching would save 50-80ms. **Deferred: smaller impact, good future candidate.**"," ","-### 5. Investigate and fix batch-round0 regression","-- **Idea**: Debug why commit 74134b4e causes the pairing benchmark to hang.","-- **Why not chosen as primary**: This is a bug fix/investigation, not a new optimization. Should be done as a prerequisite step during the Execute phase (likely a revert), not as the optimization itself.","+5. **All-or-nothing Round 0 batching (repeat previous attempt)**: The memory budget check that disabled batching entirely for large skip_domains would still apply. The pairing benchmark likely triggers the check, making the optimization a no-op. **Rejected: sub-batching directly addresses this flaw.**"]}],"originalFile":"# Task: 2026-04-11-0945-precompute-round0-logup-rules\n\nTask name: 2026-04-11-0945-precompute-round0-logup-rules\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nRound 0 constraint evaluation at APC 300 takes ~569ms — the single largest scaling bottleneck. While APC 300 has 2.15x fewer constraint instances than APC 0 (505M vs 1.13B), Round 0 at APC 300 is 3.25x SLOWER than APC 0 (569ms vs 175ms), contributing +394ms of inverse scaling.\n\nThe root cause: for each of the ~370 AIR instances with interactions (per segment, 2 segments), the Round 0 logup code path rebuilds the symbolic expression DAG and compiles GPU evaluation rules from scratch. This CPU-intensive work depends only on the AIR's constraint structure (fixed at keygen time) but is recomputed on every `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` call.\n\nProfiling data shows Round 0 GPU kernel time is ~479ms at APC 300 (baseline nsight), while total Round 0 time is 569ms post-d2h-sync. The ~90ms gap is CPU overhead: DAG construction, rule compilation, weight computation, and H2D transfers. However, the actual CPU overhead is larger because the pipeline masks some of it — GPU utilization drops when per-AIR CPU setup (0.3-0.6ms) exceeds per-AIR GPU kernel time (0.2-0.4ms for small AIRs).\n\nCompounding the issue: when batching is disabled (as for the pairing benchmark with large `skip_domain`), each AIR's DAG and rules are built TWICE:\n1. In `mod.rs` Phase 1 loop (lines 907-926) — computing rules to check batch eligibility\n2. In `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` (round0.rs lines 219-265) — rebuilding the same DAG and rules for the actual kernel launch\n\nThe Phase 1 computation is completely wasted for non-batch-eligible AIRs. This duplicate work adds ~0.2ms per AIR × 370 AIRs × 2 segments = ~148ms of pure waste.\n\nAdditionally, the Phase 2 IDFT processing loop (mod.rs lines 1184-1281) processes all 623 AIR results sequentially. Each iteration performs transpose + IDFT operations that are CPU-bound and embarrassingly parallel (each AIR writes to independent `batch_sp_poly` indices).\n\n**Note**: The batch-round0-coset-parallel commit (74134b4e, most recent) appears to cause a severe performance regression — the pairing benchmark fails to complete within 2 minutes. This commit should be investigated and likely reverted before applying this optimization. The measurements in this task reference the post-stacked-reduction state (commit 6fccffce).\n\n## Proposed Approach\n\n### Part 1: Pre-compute Round 0 logup interaction rules at keygen time\n\nIn `AirDataGpu::new()` (pkey.rs), pre-compile the Round 0 logup interaction rules alongside the existing `interaction_rules`:\n\n1. **Add `round0_interaction_rules` to `AirDataGpu`**: Build the interaction DAG with `buffer_vars=true` (as the Round 0 kernel requires) and compile rules via `SymbolicRulesGpu::new(&dag, true)`. Store the compiled rules, encoded rules on device, and a mapping from interaction index → rule index for fast weight computation.\n\n2. **Refactor `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu`** (round0.rs): Accept pre-compiled rules as a parameter instead of rebuilding from scratch. Remove the internal DAG construction and rule compilation (lines 219-265). Only compute runtime-dependent weights (numer_weights, denom_weights) using the pre-computed index mapping.\n\n3. **Simplify Phase 1 loop in `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`** (mod.rs): When `batching_enabled` is false, skip the DAG/rule construction entirely (lines 907-926) and go directly to `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` with pre-compiled rules. When batching IS enabled, use the pre-compiled rules for batch metadata instead of recompiling.\n\n### Part 2: Parallelize Phase 2 IDFT processing\n\nConvert the sequential Phase 2 loop (mod.rs lines 1184-1281) to use `rayon::par_iter()`:\n\n1. **Pre-fetch all D2H data** before entering the parallel section. For non-batched AIRs that still have `DeviceBuffer` results, call `to_host()` before the parallel loop (D2H transfers need the CUDA context).\n\n2. **Parallel IDFT processing**: Use `pending.into_par_iter()` to process all AIR results concurrently. Each iteration writes to independent indices in `batch_sp_poly` (via `entry.trace_idx`), so no synchronization is needed beyond the atomic slice access.\n\n## Expected Outcome\n\n**Round 0 improvement (APC 300)**:\n- Eliminating duplicate DAG/rule compilation: ~100-150ms savings\n  - Phase 1 loop waste (mod.rs): ~74ms/segment × 2 = 148ms\n  - Round 0 function compilation (round0.rs): savings partially masked by GPU pipeline\n- Net Round 0 reduction: ~100-150ms (from ~569ms to ~419-469ms)\n- At APC 0 (99 AIR instances): minimal savings (~5-15ms) — optimization primarily helps APC 300\n\n**Phase 2 IDFT parallelization (APC 300)**:\n- Sequential IDFT for 623 AIRs → parallel on 8+ cores\n- Expected savings: 30-50ms\n\n**Combined STARK excl trace improvement (APC 300)**:\n- Total: ~130-200ms reduction (from ~2195ms to ~1995-2065ms)\n- Scaling ratio: from ~1.014 to ~0.95-0.97 (APC 300 becomes meaningfully faster than APC 0)\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n1. **Primary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 decreases by ≥100ms vs pre-optimization baseline (2195ms → ≤2095ms)\n2. **Secondary**: Round 0 APC 300 decreases by ≥80ms (569ms → ≤489ms)\n3. **Scaling**: APC 300/APC 0 ratio for STARK excl trace ≤ 0.98 (APC 300 measurably faster)\n4. **Correctness**: All 94 openvm-cuda-backend tests pass; proof verification succeeds for APC 0/100/300\n5. **No regression at APC 0**: STARK excl trace APC 0 change ≤ ±20ms\n\n## Considered Alternatives\n\n### 1. Batch GKR input evaluation kernels\n- **Idea**: Batch the per-AIR `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` launches (791 instances at APC 300) into fewer large launches, similar to the Round 0 batching approach.\n- **Expected impact**: ~200-300ms (nsight data from 003 directory shows 5x GPU time reduction: 428ms → 86ms)\n- **Why not chosen**: Complex implementation requiring new CUDA kernels + Rust orchestration. Same memory constraint challenges as batch-round0 (which itself appears to cause a regression). The GKR input eval already uses pre-compiled rules from the proving key, so the redundant compilation savings don't apply here. Higher risk for uncertain return — the 003 directory metrics show STARK excl trace barely improved despite the GPU time reduction, suggesting hidden CPU overhead offsets.\n\n### 2. Multi-stream Round 0 pipelining\n- **Idea**: Use 2-4 CUDA streams to overlap GPU kernel execution with CPU Phase 2 IDFT processing.\n- **Expected impact**: 50-100ms savings\n- **Why not chosen**: Complex synchronization logic, risk of race conditions with VPMM memory management. The Phase 2 IDFT parallelization achieves similar savings with much less complexity (rayon vs CUDA stream management).\n\n### 3. Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels\n- **Idea**: Similar to the degenerate stacked reduction batching, batch the 7,467 per-window non-degenerate kernel launches.\n- **Expected impact**: ~50-80ms on Stacked Reduction (from 147ms)\n- **Why not chosen**: Lower absolute impact than Round 0 optimization. Stacked Reduction already improved 2x. Diminishing returns.\n\n### 4. Rayon-only Phase 2 IDFT (without rule pre-computation)\n- **Idea**: Just parallelize the Phase 2 loop without addressing the redundant compilation.\n- **Expected impact**: 30-50ms only\n- **Why not chosen**: Included as Part 2 of the chosen approach. On its own, the savings are too small to justify a full optimization iteration. Combining with Part 1 gives meaningful total impact.\n\n### 5. Investigate and fix batch-round0 regression\n- **Idea**: Debug why commit 74134b4e causes the pairing benchmark to hang.\n- **Why not chosen as primary**: This is a bug fix/investigation, not a new optimization. Should be done as a prerequisite step during the Execute phase (likely a revert), not as the optimization itself.\n"}}
